```java
class GlobalCounter {
    private final int[] counts = new int[16];
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    void increment(long key) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            counts[(int) (Math.abs(key) % counts.length)]++;
        }
    }
}
```

Every thread, regardless of which slot its key maps to, contends for the exact same lock. Under heavy concurrent load, threads spend most of their time waiting rather than doing the trivial work of incrementing one integer — the lock, not the work, is the bottleneck.

```java
class StripedCounter {
    private final int[] counts;
    private final Object[] locks;

    StripedCounter(int stripes) {
        counts = new int[stripes];
        locks = new Object[stripes];
        for (int i = 0; i < stripes; i++) locks[i] = new Object();
    }

    void increment(long key) {
        int i = (int) (Math.abs(key) % locks.length);
        synchronized (locks[i]) {
            counts[i]++;
        }
    }
}
```

Each slot has its own lock. Two threads whose keys hash to different stripes never contend at all — only threads landing on the *same* stripe serialize against each other, cutting contention roughly in proportion to the number of stripes.

```java
class LockFreeCounter {
    private final AtomicLongArray counts;

    LockFreeCounter(int slots) {
        counts = new AtomicLongArray(slots);
    }

    void increment(long key) {
        int i = (int) (Math.abs(key) % counts.length());
        counts.incrementAndGet(i);   // hardware CAS loop, no blocking at all
    }
}
```

`incrementAndGet` never blocks a thread — it reads the current value, computes the new one, and retries the compare-and-swap only if another thread updated the slot first. Under contention on the *same* slot, threads still compete, but they burn CPU cycles retrying rather than sleeping while blocked. See [lock-free and atomic object design](lock-free-and-atomic-object-design) for how far this approach extends beyond a simple counter.
